<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>false</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <WarnOn>3390;$(WarnOn)</WarnOn>
    <!-- 0.2.0 carries two things, both of them reasons a consumer must take a
         new slot rather than a re-pack of 0.1.0.

         First, `Fuaran.Program.Bounded` gained the channel connection and its
         out-of-band apply entry point. Additive: nothing existing changed shape,
         so no consumer stops compiling.

         Second, and the sharper of the two: the whole UI-tier floor moved from
         0.46.0 to 0.70.0, so every assembly here is COMPILED against a different
         tier. That is not a source change a consumer can read off a diff — the
         binding is in the emitted IL, against member signatures and DU case
         ordinals the tier reordered along the way. A consumer that took 0.1.0
         and a 0.70.0 tier got a `MissingMethodException` at the entry points,
         which is exactly what a version exists to prevent, so this is a new slot
         and never a re-pack. The one source-visible edge of it is the clipboard
         payload, which is a `TextSource` here now because the tier widened it.

         The producer-side rule underneath both: a change to a package's public
         contract — or, as here, to the contract it was BUILT against — advances
         the version in the SAME commit, because a folder feed keys on id+version
         and re-packing a slot in place swaps the contract under every consumer
         whose cache still serves the old bits. This line versions all four
         packages together, which is the repository's shape: a minor bump on an
         untouched package costs nothing, and a per-package line would have to be
         right four times. -->
    <!-- 0.4.0 is a NEW SLOT rather than a re-pack of 0.3.0 for the plainest of
         the reasons this comment block has recorded: v0.3.0 is TAGGED. A
         released slot is some consumer's contract, and this change costs them
         source edits, so it cannot be published over the top of one.

         What a consumer pays. The UI tier's action union widened
         `Navigate` — its route is a text SOURCE now rather than a literal, and
         it carries a browsing-context target — and gained two cases. So every
         positional match over the union in a consumer's own code stops
         compiling, and the client-effect vocabulary widened in step. The floor
         moves with it: the tier pins here are 0.78.1, so nothing built against
         an earlier tier links against these assemblies. That second half is the
         one a consumer cannot read off a source diff, and it is why the 0.2.0
         entry below argued the same case at length — the binding is in the
         emitted IL, against member signatures and case ordinals the tier moved.

         What it BUYS, stated because a version that only costs is one people
         defer. A bound route is now expressible: `Navigate` reads a binding at
         dispatch time, so "open the order the reader selected" is a document
         rather than a host closure. A route that fails to resolve navigates
         nowhere and says so, which is the tier's own rule and not this
         placement's invention. `Focus` reaches an effect arm the vocabulary has
         carried since it was cut and nothing on the wire could name.

         One thing deliberately did NOT move, and it is the reason this is a
         minor rather than something larger: a `Confirm` action is a documented
         no-op on the bounded path, diagnosed like every other. The round trip
         it needs — ask, answer, re-validate, then dispatch the continuation
         through its own gate — is a placement capability, not a raise, and
         claiming it here would put a version number on work nobody has done.

         This line versions all four packages together, which is the
         repository's shape: a minor bump on an untouched package costs nothing,
         and a per-package line would have to be right four times. -->
    <Version>0.4.0</Version>
    <!-- This repository's restore path is PUBLIC: it publishes to nuget.org, so
         a consumer of the published packages reaches the registry and nothing
         else. Undeclared would read as `local-feed`, under which a pin only this
         machine's folder feed can serve looks correct. `tools/check-pins.ps1`
         already enforces the same rule before every build; this declares it to
         the estate sweep as well, so a raise toward a version the producer has
         cut but not released is refused rather than reported afterwards. -->
    <FuaranRestorePath>public</FuaranRestorePath>
    <Authors>Fuaran</Authors>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
